{"id": "x0402", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 23, "onset_chunk": 21, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the first time it went off, it rang four times. The second time, it rang for three times as long as the first time, so that's 12 times. And the third time, it rang for half as long as the second time, so that's six times. Add ing them up, four plus twelve plus six equals twenty two. So the alarm rang twenty two times in all.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 23, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0410", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Kenny needs 1 00 dollars for the amusement park ticket, and he's going to sell his Pokemon cards at 1.5 dollars each. He' s keeping one third of the cards, which means he' s selling two thirds of them. After buying the ticket, he has 50 dollars left. Let' s figure out how many cards he started with. First, we know that he earned enough money from selling the cards to cover both the 1 00 dollar ticket and still have 50 dollars in cash. That means the total amount he made from selling the cards is 1 00 plus 50, which is 15 0 dollars. Since he sold each card for 1.5 dollars, we can divide the total money made by the price per card to find out how many cards he sold: 1 50 divided by 1. 5 equals 1 00 cards. But remember, these are just the cards he sold, not all of them. He kept one third, so the cards he sold represent two thirds of his original collection. If 10 0 cards are two third s, then one third would be half of that, which is 5 0 cards. So, adding the 5 0 he kept to the 1 00 he sold gives us a total of 1", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0418", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Carlos rents a canoe for 3 0 dollars an hour, and he uses it for 3 hours. So that' s 30 times 3, which is 9 0 dollars. Ben jie rent s a banana boat ra ft for 18 dollars an hour, and he uses it for 5 hours. So that's 1 8 times 5, which is 90 dollars. Now, if we add both amount s together, 9 0 plus 90 equal s 18 0 dollars. So they will pay 18 0 dollars altogether.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 27, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0426", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 20, "onset_chunk": 20, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Melissa has a total of 8 dogs, plus 5 cats, plus 3 birds, plus 12 horses. That adds up to 28 animals in all. If she wants to split them evenly over the days of the week, she' ll groom 4 animals each day.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 15, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0434", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "On Monday, Walt walked four miles. On Tuesday, he walked six times as many miles as he did on Monday, so that's 2 4 miles. His total mile age from Monday through Wednesday was 41 miles. So, adding up Monday and Tuesday, that' s 28 miles. Therefore, on Wednesday, he must have walked 1 3 miles to reach the total of 4 1.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 24, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0442", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "On the second day,", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 2, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0450", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 23, "onset_chunk": 23, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So let's break this down step by step. On the first day, there were two container s, and each had five vehicles inside. That means 2 times 5 is 10 vehicles total from the first day.Now, after more containers came in on the second day, the total number of vehicles at the port became 3 0. So, if we subtract the initial 1 0 vehicles from the total of 3 0, that leaves us with 20 vehicles added on the second day. Since each container holds 5 vehicles, we divide 2 0 by 5 to find out how many container s were brought in. 2 0 divided by 5 equal s 4. So, four containers were imported on the second day.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 46, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0458", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "A dozen cups is 12, so Judy brought 12 dishes. Her friend had 20 more dishes than Ju dy's 12, which means her friend brought 3 2 dishes. So together they brought 4 4 dishes in total. And since her friend brought 40 cups, the two of them brought 52 cup s altogether. Add ing the dishes and cups gives us a total of 96 utensils.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0466", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we have two adults and four kids. Each adult's slice is twice as big as each kid' s. Let's say a kid gets x, then an adult gets 2 x. There are four kids, so that's 4x for the kids, and two adults, so 2 times 2 x is 4x for the adults. Add them up: 4x plus 4x is 8 x total. That means each unit x is one-eighth of the watermelon. Since an adult gets 2x, that' s 2 divided by 8, which is one- fourth. One-four th is 25%.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0474", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "If Courtney said there were 4 8 people, but Kelly says that's 20% too many, then the actual number is less than 48. To find out how many actually attended, you can figure out what 100% was after accounting for the overstate ment. Since 4 8 represents 1 20%, divide 48 by 1.2. That gives you about 40 people. So, if Kelly is right, roughly 40 people went to the concert.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0482", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "There are 12 blue gumballs, so four times that would be 4 8. And then we add the extra 2 2 pink gumballs to get a total of 7 0 pink gumball s.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 10, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0490", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "April has 3 0 days, so Wat son works for 3 0 days / 5 days per week = 6 weeks. If he works 1 0 hours a day for 6 days, he works 1 0 hours/day * 6 days/week = 60 hours/week. So in total Watson worked 60 hours/week * 6 weeks = 3 6 0 hours. At $10 per hour, Wa tson earned 3 60 hours * $1 0/hour = $3 6 00. Add ing the bonus, Watson made $3 6 00 + $ 300 = $ 3 90 0 in April.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 39, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0498", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Tyson runs 5,0 00 meters every day. His coach wants him to run one- fifth times more. That means he should add one-fifth of 5,000 to his daily distance. One-fifth of 5,0 00 is 1,000, so he would run 6,0 00 meters each day. June has 30 days, so over the month, he ran 6, 000 me ters per day times 3 0 days, which equal s 180, 000 meters in total for June.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0506", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 28, "onset_chunk": 27, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So on the first day, Gun ther had 4 8 bananas. Then Arnold stole half of them, which is 24, leaving 2 4 bananas. The next day, he added 2 5 more, so that brings it to 49 banana s. But then Arnold stole another 1 2, taking it down to 37. On the third day, he added 6 more, making the total 4 3 bananas in the pile when Gunther counted them.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 25, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0514", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's break this down step by step. You are three years younger than your brother, so if his age is B, then yours is B minus 3. You're also two years older than your sister, so her age would be B minus 5. Your mom is one less than three times your brother' s age, which means her age is 3 B minus 1. Now add all the ages together: brother plus you plus sister plus mom equals 8 7. Sub stituting the expressions, that becomes B plus B minus 3 plus B minus 5 plus 3 B minus 1 equal s 87. S implifying gives us 6B minus 9 equals 8 7. Add 9 to both sides to get 6 B equals 96. Div ide by 6, and B is 1 6. So your brother is 16. That makes you 1 3, since you' re three years younger.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 45, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0522", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Gary does laundry twice a week, so that's 2 6 times a year. Each load uses 20 gallons of water, so he' s using 5 20 gallons a year. At fifteen cents per gallon, that comes to seventy-eight dollars a year for his laundry water.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 14, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0530", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 19, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let's break this down step by step. First, we need to figure out how long each group takes in total. Each group gets 2 minutes to get on stage, 6 minutes to perform, and 2 minutes to exit. So that' s 2 plus 6 plus 2, which is 10 minutes per group.Now, the concert is 2 hours long. Let's convert that to minutes: 2 hours times 6 0 minutes per hour equal s 1 2 0 minutes total. But we also have a 10-minute intermission during the concert, so we need to subtract that from the total time available for performances. That leaves us with 1 2 0 minus 1 0, which is 1 10 minutes actually available for groups to perform. Now, since each group takes 10 minutes, we divide the remaining time by the time per group: 1 10 divided by 10 equal s 1 1.So, V icky can fit 11 groups into the concert with a 10-minute intermission.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 55, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0538", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 25, "onset_chunk": 25, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "She's saved 1 20 toothpick s so far, and she needs to save a total of 200. So she still needs 80 more tooth picks. Since she saves 10 each week, it will take her 8 weeks to collect the remaining toothpicks.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 13, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0546", "pool": "hard-math", "n_q_chunks": 16, "onset_chunk": 15, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so we have two mixtures here. The first one is 1/2 pound of almonds and 1/3 pound of walnuts. Let's figure out the cost for that. Almon ds are $10 a pound, so half a pound would be half of $10, which is $5. Wal nuts are $15 a pound, so 1/3 of $15 is $5. Adding those together, $5 plus $5 equal s $10 for the first mixture. Now, let' s look at the second mixture: 1/5 pound of almonds and 1/3 pound of walnuts. For the almonds, 1/ 5 of $10 is $ 2. For the walnu ts, again, it 's $5. So, $ 2 plus $5 is $7 for the second mixture. To find out how much more the first mixture costs, we subtract the cost of the second from the first: $ 10 minus $ 7 equal s $3. So, it costs $ 3 more for the first mixture than the second one.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 58, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "x0554", "pool": "know-open", "n_q_chunks": 3, "onset_chunk": 3, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Boomer finds out she is a Cylon during the final season of Battlestar Galact ic a. The revelation comes in episode 1 3, titled \"A Disruption,\" when she begins experiencing strange glitches and memory loss. As the story progresses, she starts to question her identity and eventually confront s the truth about her nature as a Cylon. This moment is significant because it marks a turning point in her character development and adds depth to the series' exploration of identity and free will.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 33, "carrier_ended": null}
